WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:02.194
- Something went horribly wrong.

00:00:02.194 --> 00:00:04.052
I'm a worm murderer.

00:00:04.052 --> 00:00:06.847
This month I tried to live zero waste.

00:00:06.847 --> 00:00:08.559
I had no idea what I was doing,

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:09.866
but I learned along the way,

00:00:09.866 --> 00:00:12.778
from just trying not to
make any trash day to day,

00:00:12.778 --> 00:00:15.923
and from speaking to
experts along the way.

00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:18.923
(upbeat funk music)

00:00:20.736 --> 00:00:21.967
The average American makes about

00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:24.891
4.3 pounds of trash in one day.

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:28.858
And I'm going to be
trying to make no trash.

00:00:28.858 --> 00:00:31.884
Whenever I hear about where
the environment's going,

00:00:31.884 --> 00:00:32.972
I get really scared,

00:00:32.972 --> 00:00:35.972
and it makes me feel very powerless.

00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:43.042
How much waste does the average
person in LA make per month?

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:45.386
- About 150 pounds per month.

00:00:45.386 --> 00:00:46.555
- Wow.

00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:48.362
I think this is gonna
be really hard for me.

00:00:48.362 --> 00:00:52.529
And I'm not necessarily
really looking forward to it.

00:00:53.426 --> 00:00:55.395
I'm struggling with how
I'm supposed to eat,

00:00:55.395 --> 00:00:57.843
because most food comes in packages.

00:00:57.843 --> 00:01:00.002
Maybe I should try to go into the woods

00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:01.891
and forge for some nuts and berries?

00:01:01.891 --> 00:01:03.230
I'm having a hard time figuring out

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.327
where the line ends and begins.

00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:07.848
I'm eating these tacos,
and they're on plates.

00:01:07.848 --> 00:01:09.216
Maybe I'm not even
supposed to be doing this.

00:01:09.216 --> 00:01:13.923
I wash my hands, and I
unconsciously waved my hand

00:01:13.923 --> 00:01:14.946
and now I have this.

00:01:14.946 --> 00:01:17.619
I'm cranky because I've
been going hungry a lot.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:19.158
So I need to find a better solution

00:01:19.158 --> 00:01:21.009
than just eating bananas.

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:22.658
(groaning)

00:01:22.658 --> 00:01:25.667
How important is it that
we reduce our waste?

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.040
What's really at stake for us if we don't?

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.416
- We're stewards of this land,

00:01:29.416 --> 00:01:31.752
so every time we negatively
impact the system,

00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:34.462
we're actually jeopardizing our ability

00:01:34.462 --> 00:01:37.629
to breathe, drink water, consume food.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.254
- So I went to the store,

00:01:41.254 --> 00:01:44.845
and I brought my jars
and I was able to fill up

00:01:44.845 --> 00:01:47.931
with some quinoa from the bulk bins.

00:01:47.931 --> 00:01:49.688
And some other items.

00:01:49.688 --> 00:01:52.458
The nice side effect of this is that

00:01:52.458 --> 00:01:54.779
I am forced to eat very healthy.

00:01:54.779 --> 00:01:56.858
Because I'm not able to eat anything

00:01:56.858 --> 00:01:58.605
that comes in packaging.

00:01:58.605 --> 00:02:00.250
Which is most processed foods.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.149
I am realizing that being
able to do this challenge

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:04.751
is a privilege in itself.

00:02:04.751 --> 00:02:07.655
Because a lot of packaged, processed foods

00:02:07.655 --> 00:02:09.277
are the cheapest foods.

00:02:09.277 --> 00:02:12.688
And fresh produce can be really expensive.

00:02:12.688 --> 00:02:17.021
But collectively, I'm spending less money.

00:02:17.021 --> 00:02:19.758
These are the food scraps
from a stir fry I made.

00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:23.267
And I'm not going to eat them,
but I can't throw them away.

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:25.488
I'm keeping my compost in my freezer

00:02:25.488 --> 00:02:28.367
until I can figure out
what I'm gonna do with it.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:30.363
I used to think that I
needed different soaps

00:02:30.363 --> 00:02:31.553
for different things,

00:02:31.553 --> 00:02:34.146
but now I'm realizing
that soap is just soap.

00:02:34.146 --> 00:02:35.854
This is a meal I made.

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:39.064
It was harder to get all
the ingredients zero waste,

00:02:39.064 --> 00:02:40.729
but feeling pretty good.

00:02:40.729 --> 00:02:43.177
I'm here at Griffith
Park Composting Facility,

00:02:43.177 --> 00:02:46.166
on a Saturday morning,
and I'm gonna take a class

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:47.620
to learn how to compost.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.594
I learned in the workshop

00:02:48.594 --> 00:02:51.193
that a vermicompost would
be the best for an apartment

00:02:51.193 --> 00:02:52.804
that didn't have access to soil.

00:02:52.804 --> 00:02:54.971
So I set about making one.

00:02:56.538 --> 00:02:58.640
I'm starting my in home worm bin,

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.630
and I have egg shells, and
egg cartons for roughage

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:05.669
then I have my red wigglers,
and I'm very excited.

00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:06.824
- [Friend] Ahh!

00:03:06.824 --> 00:03:08.743
- (bleep) you, that's so gross.

00:03:08.743 --> 00:03:09.660
Let me see.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:11.870
- Oh my god, no.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:12.799
- [Woman In Challenge]
They're kinda cute, right?

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:13.714
- Yeah, I kinda like them.

00:03:13.714 --> 00:03:16.100
- And look, see they're burrowing down.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.638
And they're gonna eat the food scraps.

00:03:18.638 --> 00:03:20.111
This is getting easier everyday,

00:03:20.111 --> 00:03:22.454
and it's feeling really nice actually.

00:03:22.454 --> 00:03:24.102
Cause I always would feel kind of bad

00:03:24.102 --> 00:03:26.053
about all the trash that I was using,

00:03:26.053 --> 00:03:28.744
and it's awesome to like,

00:03:28.744 --> 00:03:31.066
put that feeling into action,

00:03:31.066 --> 00:03:33.023
and actually do something about it.

00:03:33.023 --> 00:03:35.023
Another zero waste meal.

00:03:35.925 --> 00:03:37.933
Where does the trash in LA go?

00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:41.183
- The trash goes to a transfer station,

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:45.668
where it is sorted and
then it is trucked out

00:03:45.668 --> 00:03:47.335
to a local landfill.

00:03:51.466 --> 00:03:53.725
- So how many pounds of
waste does Sunshine Canyon

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:54.984
take in per day?

00:03:54.984 --> 00:03:58.164
- About 8,500 tons of
rough use in per day.

00:03:58.164 --> 00:03:58.997
- Wow.

00:03:58.997 --> 00:04:00.860
- The majority of that comes
from the city of Los Angeles.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.480
And there's quite a few other
facilities around the area

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.495
that handle the remaining 70%.

00:04:04.495 --> 00:04:06.331
If you think of the old
garbage dump, right?

00:04:06.331 --> 00:04:07.455
That people refer to.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:08.796
It's nothing like that anymore.

00:04:08.796 --> 00:04:10.176
These are highly engineered.

00:04:10.176 --> 00:04:11.883
- Why does a landfill have to close,

00:04:11.883 --> 00:04:15.249
and what happens to a
landfill once it closes?

00:04:15.249 --> 00:04:17.409
- The landfill will reach
a certain elevation,

00:04:17.409 --> 00:04:18.995
where then it can be closed

00:04:18.995 --> 00:04:21.252
and put back as best as it can, right?

00:04:21.252 --> 00:04:22.612
To the way it was before.

00:04:22.612 --> 00:04:25.689
Unfortunately, the trash is in there.

00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:26.535
- I'm done.

00:04:26.535 --> 00:04:28.443
And I feel very happy, sad.

00:04:28.443 --> 00:04:30.010
I made this in a month.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.822
Tags from reusable bags.

00:04:31.822 --> 00:04:32.655
Some Advil.

00:04:32.655 --> 00:04:34.045
Tags from the Mason Jar.

00:04:34.045 --> 00:04:35.134
A delicious drum stick.

00:04:35.134 --> 00:04:36.259
A teabag.

00:04:36.259 --> 00:04:38.141
This was like what the socks came in.

00:04:38.141 --> 00:04:38.974
Floss.

00:04:38.974 --> 00:04:40.498
I couldn't reuse this part.

00:04:40.498 --> 00:04:44.383
What can an average person
do to reduce their trash?

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:46.319
- Reduce, reuse, recycle.

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.291
- We all like to buy
things packaged real nice,

00:04:48.291 --> 00:04:49.422
knowing no on touched 'em.

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:51.951
The reality that is creating
a lot of extra packaging.

00:04:51.951 --> 00:04:53.638
- [Bonny] We need people
to start thinking about,

00:04:53.638 --> 00:04:55.774
is the disposable product
that they're using

00:04:55.774 --> 00:04:57.870
really adding that value to their life?

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.461
- I think one person
can make a difference.

00:05:00.461 --> 00:05:01.822
- One person can really evaluate

00:05:01.822 --> 00:05:03.142
everything that they're consuming,

00:05:03.142 --> 00:05:04.761
and make some changes.

00:05:04.761 --> 00:05:06.887
You don't have to give up everything,

00:05:06.887 --> 00:05:08.523
but start with one thing.

00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:12.878
And what I've noticed is it
spreads throughout your choices.

00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:16.461
(ambient electronic music)

